  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/parambassis-lala-bred/",
      "html": "<p>The dwarf glass perch, Parambassis lala, is widely distributed in South Asia; it can be found in India, Burma and Nepal.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/parambassis-lala-bred/",
      "html": "<p>Concerning the water (pH, hardness) dwarf glassfish are undemanding; the temperature can be between 16 and 30°C (depending on the season, no sudden temperature changes!).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/parambassis-lala-bred/",
      "html": "<p>They eat exclusively frozen and live food; dry food is strictly refused.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/parambassis-lala-bred/",
      "html": "<p>Dwarf glassfish are delightful aquarium inhabitants, always \"strutting\" around the tank like little tin soldiers. The males court the females spiritedly, but without becoming aggressive.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/parambassis-lala-bred/",
      "html": "<p>Spawning takes place in fine plants; brood care is not practiced. Dwarf glassfish are rarely bred in Germany, because the feeding reflex of the larvae is only triggered by the nauplii of a certain small crustacean species (Diaptomus). Without this food, an attempt at rearing is pretty much futile.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/parambassis-lala-bred/",
      "html": "<p>In the past this dwarf, which never grows larger than 2-3 cm, was mistaken for the juvenile of other species, which is why completely wrong size data (up to 8 cm) and also completely wrong ecological data (supposedly it is a brackish water fish, but this is not true) can be found in the literature. In reality P. lala lives only in pure fresh water and remains small.</p><p>However, there are several forms of this species, which may yet turn out to be separate species upon closer inspection. Wild-caught specimens are occasionally received from India, and captive-bred specimens from Southeast Asia.</p>"
    }
  ],
